`?` postfix on `Option[T]`
==========================

The postfix `?` operator on an `Option[T]` value either
unwraps the `Some(v)` payload as the expression's result, or
early-returns `None` from the enclosing function. The
desugaring is `expr?` ≡ `match expr { Some(v) -> v; None ->
return None }`.

This file pins the happy-path unwrap and the early-return
exit on the simplest carrier shape.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    enum TpoOpt[T]
        Some(value: T)
        None

Happy path — `?` unwraps `Some`
-------------------------------

When every step succeeds, the chained `?` unwraps each
payload and the function returns the post-arithmetic `Some`.

    parse_pair_sum(a: TpoOpt[int], b: TpoOpt[int]) -> TpoOpt[int]
        val x = a?
        val y = b?
        return Some(x + y)

    #test
    test_try_some_some() -> unit
        val r = parse_pair_sum(Some(10), Some(32))
        val v = r match
            Some(n) -> n
            None    -> -1
        assert(v == 42, s"10 + 32 should be 42, got $v")

Early return — first `None` short-circuits
------------------------------------------

When the first arg is `None`, the `a?` early-returns `None`
before `b` is even read. The second arg's value is
irrelevant — even a `Some(b)` wouldn't be looked at because
control left the function before the `b?` line.

    #test
    test_try_none_short_circuits() -> unit
        val r = parse_pair_sum(None, Some(5))
        val ok = r match
            Some(_) -> false
            None    -> true
        assert(ok, "first-arg None should short-circuit to None")

Second-position early return
----------------------------

When the first arg succeeds but the second is `None`, the
second `?` is what early-returns. The first `?`'s effect
(binding `x = 7`) is observable until then, but the function
still ends with `None`.

    #test
    test_try_some_none() -> unit
        val r = parse_pair_sum(Some(7), None)
        val ok = r match
            Some(_) -> false
            None    -> true
        assert(ok, "second-arg None should short-circuit to None")

`?` in a longer chain
---------------------

Three `?` operators in sequence — confirms the desugaring is
purely local and composes.

    sum_three(a: TpoOpt[int], b: TpoOpt[int], c: TpoOpt[int]) -> TpoOpt[int]
        val x = a?
        val y = b?
        val z = c?
        return Some(x + y + z)

    #test
    test_try_three_all_some() -> unit
        val r = sum_three(Some(1), Some(2), Some(3))
        val v = r match
            Some(n) -> n
            None    -> -1
        assert(v == 6, s"1+2+3 should be 6, got $v")

    #test
    test_try_three_middle_none() -> unit
        val r = sum_three(Some(1), None, Some(3))
        val ok = r match
            Some(_) -> false
            None    -> true
        assert(ok, "middle-arg None should short-circuit")
